New scenarios (brother it.each; original 5 rows untouched):
  delivers a durably reported managed result after restart to a busy requester (restart: true)
  delivers a durably reported managed result after restart to a deleted requester (restart: true)
Both titles match the G executor selector (restart: true), so scripts/agent-work/test-runtime.sh
needed no change.

busy: the fake provider withholds its answer to the requester's 4th request until the child's
result is durable in managed_tasks.result_json AND the wake is parked as a session system event
(contextKey task:<id>), so the report lands mid-turn. Asserts the 4th request carries no
continuation text (no mid-turn injection), the pending queue row stays parked (1), and after a
gateway restart the same session receives exactly one continuation (7 requester requests total:
inspect, resolve, final), one managed_task_handlings row with the complete decision, zero extra
requests after a replayed report during the admitted turn (replayedAfterTurnStart), and the
requester session list still exactly [agent:ingenieria:managed-requester].
Run: node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server.managed-task-requester.e2e.test.ts
  --testNamePattern 'to a busy requester'  -> exit 0, 1 passed | 6 skipped.
Raw log: t2-busy-reported-green.log

deleted: after the requester turn completes, the session entry agent:ingenieria:managed-requester
is removed with applySessionEntryLifecycleMutation (same mechanism as the registry deleted case;
sessions.delete was rejected because it cancels the live child run). Then the child reports.
Asserts the requester stays at 4 requests across the restart, zero sessions for the requester
agent (no replacement conversation), the delivery row consumed terminally (pending 0; status
failed or purged) instead of parked, the result retained byte-identical in managed_tasks,
zero managed_task_handlings (blocked), and exactly 1 child report.
Run: --testNamePattern 'to a deleted requester'  -> exit 0. Raw log: t2-deleted-reported-green.log

